1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,180
In Babergh and Mid Suffolk, in eastern
England, historic roofs do not all look alike.

2
00:00:05,530 --> 00:00:08,410
Some rise steeply. Others
sit noticeably lower.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:16,230
Older timber-framed buildings here were probably roofed with thatch
or oak shingles, depending on which materials were available.

4
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:22,920
Longstraw thatch, the most traditional covering in these
districts, is laid at a steep pitch of about fifty degrees.

5
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:27,660
Eventually, clay peg tiles replaced
some of these thatched roofs.

6
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:32,770
Plain clay tile roofs are fairly steep
too, typically forty-five degrees or more.

7
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:35,983
Materials also began to travel further.

8
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:43,203
From the eighteenth century, clay pantiles were used more
commonly, as moving materials around the country became easier.

9
00:00:43,553 --> 00:00:49,473
In the nineteenth century, the railways meant that slate
from Wales, Cornwall and Cumbria was often used here.

10
00:00:50,342 --> 00:00:52,042
Slate behaves differently.

11
00:00:52,392 --> 00:00:59,712
Slate roofs here are usually pitched at around thirty degrees,
and slate is more effective than smaller tiles at a lower pitch.

12
00:01:00,592 --> 00:01:03,752
Thatch, clay tile and slate
each have a typical pitch.

13
00:01:04,102 --> 00:01:08,632
These are tendencies, not rules, and not
every building followed the same path.

14
00:01:08,982 --> 00:01:12,442
But the pitch of an old roof can
still be a clue to its covering.
